<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hatshepsut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1479–1458 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hatshepsut was a female <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> who ruled c. 1479–1458 BCE. Her reign was marked by building works such as her mortuary temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a> and by the expedition to the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>. She is one of the best-known rulers of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hatshepsut.jpg" alt="Hatshepsut" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seated_Statue_of_Hatshepsut_MET_Hatshepsut2012_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hatshepsut</b>, who ruled as pharaoh alongside the young Thutmose III from approximately 1479 to 1458 BCE, consolidated this inheritance through trade rather than war, dispatching the celebrated expedition to Punt and sponsoring an extraordinary building programme at Karnak and Deir el-Bahari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…speak of the Keftiu, usually taken to be the Cretans, and paintings in the Theban tombs of officials of <b>Hatshepsut</b> and Thutmose III in the 15th century BCE, among them Senenmut and Rekhmire, show men with long locks of hair and patterned kilts bringing Aegean vessels as gifts.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hatshepsut</b> ruled Egypt as king; Shajar al-Durr ruled Egypt as sultan in 1250; Razia Sultana reigned in Delhi from 1236 to 1240; Tribhuwana and Suhita reigned in their own right in Majapahit; Yohl Ik'nal held the full title of holy lord at Palenque from 583 to 604 among…</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>The throne usually passed from father to son, but <b>Hatshepsut</b> and Tausret ruled as kings in their own right.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>The best-known record, the reliefs of <b>Hatshepsut's</b> temple at Deir el-Bahari (c. 1470 BCE), show houses on stilts, domed huts, a ruler called Parahu and his queen Ati, and myrrh trees being loaded in baskets for replanting in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a></li></ul></section>
